I’ll first establish the measurement details, inspect the pattern and the available refinement/search tooling, then build a candidate ledger before refining anything. I’ll keep phase trials on a common baseline and require residual-peak closure before producing the GSAS-II project and deliverables.

The chemistry narrows the first inventory to Mn–W–O products plus residual WO₃/Mn oxides, but I won’t assume MnWO₄ is the only product. I’m now checking the frozen structure index and extracting peak families so candidates can be screened by whole-pattern evidence, including off-stoichiometric Mn–W oxides and analogue-derived prototypes.

Peak-family mapping now supports two clear nominations: wolframite MnWO₄ accounts for the dominant family, while monoclinic WO₃ predicts the otherwise coherent 20–35° residual family (notably near 23.50°, 26.94°, 33.66°, and 34.52° after the common displacement). I’m moving to controlled GSAS-II branches across every available WO₃ structure; Mn-oxide precursors remain pending until the two-phase residual is evaluated.

The WO₃ screen is decisive at the topology level: low-symmetry room-temperature WO₃ branches reduce the common-baseline Rwp from ~32.4% to 27.4–27.8% with stable 16–17 wt% contributions; tetragonal/hexagonal branches give only marginal gains or implausible scaling. Triclinic, monoclinic, and alternate-cell monoclinic WO₃ remain a close structural shortlist, so I’m checking their cell distortions/provenance and then will map residuals for a third phase.

The shortlist refinements are still running because each branch is replaying the same three GSAS-II stages. The provisional fractions are consistent across the physically plausible WO₃ models (~83–84% MnWO₄, ~16–17% WO₃), which is encouraging; the remaining decision is whether the observed peak splitting genuinely supports lower symmetry or merely rewards extra cell degrees of freedom.

The residual is not consistent with a simple “leftover Mn₃O₄ only” story. Its strongest low-angle family (20.32/20.88°) and companion peaks near 35.62°, 47.01°, and 54.90° closely match an Fe₂WO₆-type parent after Mn substitution, making Mn₂WO₆ a falsifiable off-stoichiometric candidate. I’m constructing symmetry-preserving Mn₂WO₆ analogues across the available parent topologies and screening them alongside every available Mn₃O₄/Mn₂O₃/MnO polymorph.

The analogue branches are giving a warning sign rather than closure: Fe₂WO₆- and Y₂WO₆-derived Mn₂WO₆ models each take a positive 5–7 wt% scale and lower Rwp to ~17.40%, but they leave the main residual family essentially intact and introduce strong negative peaks elsewhere. I’m treating them as tested but not accepted, and broadening the search to W suboxides and alternative tungstate topologies before deciding whether the residual is one missing phase or multiple precursor-derived phases.

The residual search has now surfaced a much stronger third-phase hypothesis: an orthorhombic Mn₃O₄ superstructure derived from a charge-ordered Fe₃O₄ prototype reproduces the unexplained peak family far better than the tested Mn₂WO₆ analogues. I’m putting that hypothesis through the same controlled GSAS-II branch test now, alongside the conventional Mn₃O₄ structures, before allowing it into the inventory.

The visual Fe₃O₄ match needs one important qualification: the 2107249 database entry expands to an implausibly sparse Mn₃O₄ model (1.43 Å contact and some atoms with no neighbor within 3.19 Å). I will not fit that defective transcription as though it were valid. The other charge-ordered Fe₃O₄ entries preserve the same topology with physically sensible 1.79–2.05 Å nearest-neighbor distances, so those—plus the exact Mn₃O₄ polymorphs—are the falsifiable tests.

The controlled branches rule out conventional tetragonal Mn₃O₄ as a meaningful contributor (~0.3 wt%) and show only marginal gains for the physical orthorhombic Mn₃O₄ models. The remaining residual is still too coherent to stop. I’m checking the synthetic-pattern construction rules and the broad prototype library next, because the peak family may belong to a non-obvious substituted oxide rather than an unreacted precursor.

The residual lines are not reflections of the fitted wolframite MnWO₄, and the fitted WO₃ can account for only a few of them. Their corrected sequence is consistent enough with a separate manganese-oxide lattice that I’m extending the controlled branch test to all exact Mn₂O₃ structures; those had only received a coarse screen so far. This is also a useful check against mistaking texture for a phase.

Indexing resolves the main residual family: it is tetragonal trirutile-type Mn₂WO₆. A cell near a = 4.394 Å, c = 8.917 Å predicts the observed 20.324, 29.137, 35.635, 46.6, 59.91, 64.88, 70.65, and 74.86° lines as one consistent sequence. The earlier trirutile branch was inconclusive because its Cr₂WO₆ parent started 5% away in a; its scale collapsed before the cell could move. I’m rebuilding that same topology at the indexed metric and repeating the branch from the unchanged baseline.

The indexed metric confirms the missing chemistry is compatible with Mn₂WO₆, but unconstrained trirutile coordinate refinement reaches the right intensities only by creating sub-ångström contacts, so that branch is invalid and has been rolled back. I’m now doing the symmetry-diverse part of the search: all A₂BO₆/AB₂O₆ oxide prototypes in the frozen library, mapped to Mn₂WO₆ by site multiplicity and ranked against the same residual. This should separate a genuine alternative topology from a trirutile intensity coincidence.

The topology-diverse branches are decisive: the Cu₂TeO₆-derived P2₁/c model gives Rwp 15.84% and a stable 9.5 wt% contribution, whereas the next-best orthorhombic model remains at 18.19% and the other shortlisted topologies collapse or diverge. Its refined cell is only modestly changed in the equivalent GSAS setting and retains sensible Mn–O/W–O distances (minimum 1.875 Å). I’m now converging the three-phase model and separating common instrumental width from phase-specific size/strain before touching atomic coordinates.

After refinement of only the physically interpretable heavy-atom coordinates, the same tetragonal residual subset remains (29.14, 35.64, 47.01, 59.92, 64.88, 70.65, 74.86°). That means the monoclinic Mn₂WO₆ analogue is not merely a distorted surrogate for the indexed trirutile pattern. I’m testing the falsifiable four-phase inventory—adding a separate P4₂/mnm Mn₂WO₆ polymorph to the now-stable model—before any oxygen-coordinate refinement.

I’ve narrowed the inventory to MnWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and two distinct Mn₂WO₆ structure types. I’m now validating the last coordinate-refinement branch and will retain it only if the geometry remains chemically credible; after that I’ll replay and export the exact final project.

The oxygen-coordinate branch lowered Rwp, but it is decisively nonphysical (W–O ≈1.21 Å and Mn–O ≈1.26 Å), so I’m rejecting it. The preceding free Mn-coordinate branch also drives Mn–O down to ≈1.68 Å; I’m rolling that back as overfit and rebuilding the four-phase model from the last chemically credible checkpoint.

The residual family is not adequately explained by the trace trirutile phase alone. I’m broadening the search from exact Mn/W entries to all physically plausible two-cation oxide prototypes, mapping parent cation sites onto Mn and W and enforcing charge-balance and contact-distance screens before any GSAS-II trial.

The broad prototype screen is still running over several thousand oxide structures. In parallel with that evidence, the current best credible model remains at Rwp ≈14.6%; I’m treating the lower-Rwp coordinate fits as invalid rather than letting them mask the unresolved phase family.

The screen is computationally heavier than the formula-specific passes, but it is progressing without errors. I’m keeping the search constrained to charge-balanced Mn/W/O compositions and physically connected frameworks so the shortlist remains chemically testable.

The broad screen found a decisive match: the one-substitution parent Mn₃TeO₆ (COD 2211528), with Te→W, predicts the unexplained peaks at ~20.3, 28.6–29.1, 32.2, 35.2–35.6, 46.2, 54.7–55.6, and 66.7°. This is a much more economical explanation than the two provisional Mn₂WO₆ additions, so I’m testing Mn₃WO₆ from a clean two-phase checkpoint and will remove the provisional phases if their scales collapse.

The Mn₃WO₆ branch is decisive: releasing only its symmetry-independent cell terms dropped Rwp from 18.2% to 6.69% at ~17 wt%, with modest cell changes and intact geometry. I’m now separating phase broadening from the previously inflated common instrument width; this step is slower because each cycle rebuilds all three phase profiles.

The phase-specific strain model reduces Rwp to 4.15%, and the fractions have stabilized near 70.1% MnWO₄, 14.8% WO₃, and 15.1% Mn₃WO₆. One Mn₃WO₆ strain parameter crossed sign (mathematically equivalent in the profile, but not reportable), so I’ll normalize it to a positive fixed value after completing the width separation.

A controlled anisotropy screen supports one modest uniaxial strain term on MnWO₄ (axis [100], components ~6080/6960 ppm), improving Rwp to 4.81% without boundary behavior. The other anisotropy branches give small gains or inflated values, so I’m retaining only this one and moving to symmetry-limited coordinate checks.

The three-phase model is now stable: MnWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and an analogue-derived trigonal Mn₃WO₆ phase. I’m checking the last symmetry-limited coordinate refinement for physical bond geometry, then I’ll run closure/control branches and package the exact replayable GSAS-II result.

The Mn₃WO₆ manganese refinement is physically acceptable: the largest independent shift is about 0.06 Å, Mn–O remains 2.13–2.34 Å, and W–O remains 1.91–1.92 Å. It also lowers Rwp from 4.23% to 4.09%, so I’m retaining it and testing the two symmetry-independent oxygen sites as the final coordinate-complexity increment.

The oxygen branch stays chemically sound, but its improvement is small and the first pass ended with a marginal shift/esd. I’m replaying that same six-parameter refinement from its own endpoint; if it does not settle cleanly, I’ll roll back to the manganese-only model rather than overfit it.

The oxygen coordinates replayed cleanly (shift/esd 0.26), so they are supported and retained. Releasing only symmetry-independent cells then reduced Rwp to 3.99%; I’m replaying that cell step because its first pass had not fully settled in parameter-space even though the least-squares objective had converged.

The residual map is dominated by alternating positive/negative lobes around already modeled strong peaks, which points to profile/position mismatch rather than a new peak family. For the required topology control, I’m now comparing the accepted R-3 Mn₃WO₆ analogue against physically valid Pa-3 and P2₁/c Mn₃WO₆ constructions from the identical two-phase baseline; a P1 construction was rejected before fitting because it contains a 0.68 Å contact.

Closure controls do not support another crystalline phase: a trirutile Mn₂WO₆ analogue contributes only 0.020 wt%, and Mn₃O₄ only 0.036 wt% while worsening the fit; both are removed. A second Mn₂WO₆ trial became numerically singular after its scale collapsed, so it is logged as unknown rather than falsely “rejected.” I’m now testing one sample-displacement term, with the instrument zero fixed, to see whether the paired peak-position residuals can be reduced without introducing a correlated zero/shift pair.

The one-term displacement refinement settles at only +4.5 μm and improves Rwp by 0.012 percentage point, but the inherited effective zero is 0.425°, outside the known instrumental range. I’m therefore testing an equivalent, physically interpretable parameterization: fix zero at 0° and transfer the offset into an initialized sample displacement near −740 μm, then refine displacement plus cells. Only one positional nuisance term remains active.

The physically reparameterized zero/shift branch converges near −700 μm, but it worsens Rwp to 4.06% and remains coupled to cell scale. I’m rejecting that decomposition and retaining the simpler fixed effective angular offset, explicitly treating it as a combined zero/displacement correction rather than a calibrated instrument zero. The final nuisance screen now tests one March–Dollase texture term at a time from the same stable project.

The texture screen finds one modest, non-boundary effect for WO₃ along [010] (March–Dollase ratio 0.982; Rwp 3.971% versus 3.993%). Other axes/phases either improve less or refine essentially to 1.000. I’m retaining only this term provisionally and replaying it by phase identity before a final cell pass.

The WO₃ texture term replayed stably and remains modest (0.981). A separate polarization check produces a much larger global intensity improvement, lowering Rwp to 3.61%; I’m checking that its value lies inside the measurement model and replaying it before deciding whether it is identifiable enough to retain.

Polarization converges reproducibly at a physical value (about 0.788), and the alternating cell/texture/polarization passes are now stable at Rwp 3.604%. The WO₃ texture ratio adjusts slightly to 0.978 but remains mild. I’m doing one final low-angle roughness control; it will be retained only if both parameters are physical and the improvement is stable.

The roughness test is rejected: a zero start drops one parameter as singular, while a physical seeded start makes A and B 100% correlated. The simpler model is retained. I’m now performing the final residual/structure audit and generating the root deliverables directly from the exact project state.

The final artifacts are generated. The accepted project replays atomically with the same fractions and Rwp, does not mutate during replay, and raises no negative-width/background/profile review flags. I’m running the last consistency checks now: root CIF/project correspondence, fraction normalization, fit-grid identity, and ledger/report completeness.

Completed and verified by an independent atomic replay of the saved GSAS-II project.

- MnWO₄, P 2/c: 69.53 wt%
- WO₃, P 2₁/c: 15.00 wt%
- Mn₃WO₆, R -3: 15.47 wt%
- Rwp: 3.604%; Rp: 2.825%; GOF: 2.033

Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline phases. Mn₃WO₆ is strongly supported by phase closure but uses an Mn₃TeO₆-derived analogue structure; its exact oxygen coordinates remain less certain. Conservative fraction uncertainty is approximately ±1–2 percentage points.

